  "summary": "The Oakland City Council voted to approve a $2.25 million, two-year contract with Flock Safety to continue operating nearly 300 ALPR cameras and add 40 new PTZ cameras, with Councilmember Carroll Fife the sole dissent. The contract includes amendments restricting data sharing with federal, out-of-state, and multi-state agencies, barring use for immigration enforcement or investigating reproductive/gender-affirming care, and imposing penalties up to $200,000 for violations.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Oakland police will continue using a mass surveillance network of nearly 300 cameras to track vehicles throughout the city under a $2.25 million contract approved by the City Council on Tuesday.",
      "paragraph_idx": 7
    },
    {
      "quote": "Wang and Brown's amendments, in part, prohibit Flock from allowing the federal government or any out-of-state agencies to access data from Oakland's cameras, and they prevent Flock from allowing Oakland's data to show up in any national or multi-state searches.",
      "paragraph_idx": 22
    },
    {
      "quote": "Urquiza told the council that OPD has shared Flock data with 50 other law enforcement agencies so far.",
      "paragraph_idx": 17
    },
    {
      "quote": "The amendments approved Tuesday require Oakland police to do a public search for the next ALPR vendor while the two-year contract is underway.",
      "paragraph_idx": 35
    }
  ],
